WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.820
Has "The Simpsons" predicted the future once&nbsp;
again? Some fans think the events of a 2006&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.560
episode are eerily similar to the real-life&nbsp;
2023 catastrophe of the Titan submersible.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.280
Fans have often noticed that "The&nbsp;
Simpsons" has eerily predicted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.379
lot of things that have ended up coming&nbsp;
true. For one, Donald Trump's presidency.

00:00:18.379 --> 00:00:22.099
"As you know, we've inherited quite a&nbsp;
budget crunch from President Trump."

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:26.700
The show is also thought to have predicted several&nbsp;
different Super Bowl outcomes, smart watches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.440
Sigfried getting attacked by his own tiger&nbsp;
during a show, the FIFA corruption scandal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.080
and Donald Trump running for president&nbsp;
again in 2024. The list goes on and on. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.200
fans are saying that the long-running&nbsp;
animated series also predicted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.280
Titan submersible imploding underwater while&nbsp;
searching for the wreckage of the Titanic.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.380
In the Season 17 episode "Homer's Paternity&nbsp;
Coot," Homer thinks he's found his "real" father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.180
a guy named Mason Fairbanks, who's voiced&nbsp;
by guest star Michael York. Inexplicably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.500
the two set out to find underwater treasure in&nbsp;
the wreckage of a ship called the "Piso Mojado"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.820
which is Spanish for "wet floor." Homer,&nbsp;
predictably, gets stuck in the wreckage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.440
He loses oxygen in the deep ocean and wakes&nbsp;
up in a hospital a few days later — meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.700
a very different fate than the five people&nbsp;
aboard the Titan submersible. Yet fans took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.601
to Twitter to claim that, once again, "The&nbsp;
Simpsons" seems to have predicted the future.

00:01:15.601 --> 00:01:20.280
On the one hand, "The Simpsons" has been on&nbsp;
the air for so long — since December 1989,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.580
to be specific — that it feels like an&nbsp;
inevitability that the show would end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.960
predicting some real-life events by coincidence.&nbsp;
And that seems to be the case with all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.060
instances fans have noticed. That said, it's still&nbsp;
freaking fans out how similar this instance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.680
to the real-life tragedy. One fan on Twitter&nbsp;
wrote, before the determination was made that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.101
the sub imploded and experts were thinking the&nbsp;
people inside were running out of breathable air:

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:45.540
"The Simpsons really predicted the&nbsp;
titanic submarine situation .. and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.208
that they would completely run out of&nbsp;
oxygen [...] this is actually scary."

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:52.301
Another was clearly unsettled,&nbsp;
making the simple proclamation:

00:01:52.301 --> 00:01:53.801
"we live in a simulation."

00:01:53.801 --> 00:01:57.840
But it's worth pointing out how different the&nbsp;
real-life incident was from the story of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.680
Simpsons" episode. The Titan submersible&nbsp;
embarked on its journey to explore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.502
wreckage of the Titanic on Sunday, June&nbsp;
18, 2023, with five passengers aboard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.502 --> 00:02:11.820
OceanGate founder Stockton Rush, Hamish&nbsp;
Harding, Paul-Henri Nargeolet, Shahzada Dawood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.080
and Dawood's 19-year-old son Suleman. The&nbsp;
problems started pretty much immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.640
with the craft's communications failing within&nbsp;
the first two hours of the voyage. On Thursday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.680
June 22, officials determined that the five&nbsp;
passengers had perished, likely in an implosion.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.940
In a press conference after an extensive&nbsp;
search, Rear Admiral John Mauger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.067
who serves as the commander of the First Coast&nbsp;
Guard District, delivered the sobering news.

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:40.320
"The debris is consistent with a&nbsp;
catastrophic implosion of the vessel."

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.340
The experts believe that the implosion happened&nbsp;
shortly after communications were lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:48.900
especially after they found debris from the&nbsp;
Titan itself near the Titanic's wreckage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.500
Despite the differences, another Twitter&nbsp;
user seemed to feel personally targeted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.640
by the similarities to the "Simpsons" episode,&nbsp;
writing before the Thursday press conference,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.260
"every new detail of this story feels like it&nbsp;
was engineered to drive me specifically insane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.420
mike reiss, previous visitor to titanic wreckage&nbsp;
and author of simpsons episodes 'moaning lisa'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:11.460
'stark raving dad' and 'the way we was', says&nbsp;
on chance for survivors he's 'not optimistic.'"

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.540
That's right: Mike Reiss, former showrunner&nbsp;
and writer on "The Simpsons," has a personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.800
connection to the expedition. On June 19,&nbsp;
he told "BBC Breakfast" that he wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.634
hopeful about the fate of the passengers&nbsp;
on board, and he knew better than most.

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:29.386
"I don't know how anyone's going to be able&nbsp;
to access it, much less bring it back up."

00:03:29.386 --> 00:03:33.480
Reiss has actually gone on three separate&nbsp;
expeditions with OceanGate, the company that made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.400
the Titan, and he knows that their safety measures&nbsp;
weren't always great from personal experience. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.501
even said that, during all of his dives, the&nbsp;
crafts lost communication with ground control.

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:45.062
"I got on the sub and at the&nbsp;
back of my mind was 'well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.062 --> 00:03:48.480
I may never get off this thing,' that's&nbsp;
always with you. [...] You have to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.254
remember the early days of the space&nbsp;
program or the early days of aviation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.254 --> 00:03:55.374
where you just make a lot of mistakes on&nbsp;
the way to figuring out what you're doing."

00:03:55.374 --> 00:03:59.280
There's still a lot of unanswered questions&nbsp;
where the Titan is concerned — and many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.480
those answers might never come. In any case, "The&nbsp;
Simpsons" has the weirdest possible connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.707
to this entire situation, and its prediction&nbsp;
streak continues, at least in the eyes of fans.

